    "payload": {
      "content": "When reviewing the CLAR dimension for papers analyzing learning dynamics in self-supervised learning (e.g., BYOL, SimSiam), pay close attention to the consistency of mathematical notation, particularly for empirical kernels like the NTK. A common weakness is the mismatch between the formal definition of a kernel (e.g., $\\hat{\\Theta}_t(x, \\mathcal{X})$) and its usage in subsequent text or equations (e.g., $\\hat{\\Theta}_t(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{X})$). Additionally, ensure that complex figures visualizing dynamics (such as eigenvalue trajectories or neural updates) have captions that explicitly describe the content of each row/panel and that axis labels are legible. Ambiguity in distinguishing between dataset-level vectors (e.g., calligraphic $\\mathcal{Z}$) and single-sample vectors (e.g., $z$) can hinder reproducibility and understanding of decoupled dynamics derivations.",
      "reviewer_dimension": "CLAR",
      "source": "a1183db720fe87be3b06a24097874fcd207b5eae4513e4317d94366712d7fbc2",
      "topic": "CLAR Review: Notation Consistency and Figure Caption Clarity in SSL Dynamics Analysis"
    },
